# EVERY HOLDER SHARE ON A LAYER, TOTALLED, WITH WHAT WOULD SUSPEND THE TOTAL. One row per layer
# naming at least one holder: how many, how many left the share unstated, the sum of the stated
# ones at the mode, and the distinct units they were quoted in.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ THREE FILES COMPUTED THIS γ SEPARATELY AND TWO OF THEM DISAGREED ABOUT ONE DOCUMENT.
#    arithmetic/shares_sum.sqlc, arithmetic/shares_bounded.sqlc and checks/shares_do_not_sum.sqlc
#    each grouped entries/holders.sqlc by (filing, layer) for the same two numbers. The two
#    arithmetic sites report an unstated share as `suspended` and a foreign unit as
#    `not comparable`; the check had only a `HAVING` for the first and NO branch at all for the
#    second, so it would have summed across units and compared the total to |r|.
#
# ⛔⛔ H IS A DISTRIBUTION OVER THE MAGNITUDE OF `r`, SO A SHARE IN ANOTHER UNIT IS NOT A SMALLER
#    SHARE, IT IS NOT A SHARE OF THIS. That is why `share_units` is carried rather than assumed
#    away: a caller compares it against its own layer's unit and decides. arithmetic/roster.sqlc
#    records `guarded_by = NULL` for shares_sum, meaning no unit rule stands there at all, which
#    made the check the only thing between that site and a wrong number.
#
# ⭐ `sum()` SKIPS NULLS SILENTLY, so a layer where half the holders stated a share sums to half
#   the truth and looks like an ordinary number. `unstated` is the column that makes that
#   visible, and every caller has to read it before it reads the sum.
-- entries/holders.sqlc folded to one row per layer.
SELECT h.filing, h.layer,
       count(*)                                     AS holders,
       count(*) FILTER (WHERE h.share_mode IS NULL)  AS unstated,
       sum(h.share_low)                              AS shares_low,
       sum(h.share_mode)                             AS shares_mode,
       sum(h.share_high)                             AS shares_high,
       array_agg(DISTINCT h.share_unit)              AS share_units
FROM (
    :compose(entries/holders.sqlc)
) h
GROUP BY h.filing, h.layer
